{
  "version": 1,
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "intro",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "# Lesson 2: Registers & MOV\n\nThe 8086 has 8 general-purpose 16-bit registers: **AX, BX, CX, DX, SI, DI, BP, SP**.\nAX/BX/CX/DX can be split into 8-bit halves (AH/AL, BH/BL, CH/CL, DH/DL)."
    },
    {
      "id": "code-1",
      "kind": "code",
      "source": ".CODE\nMOV AX, 1234h    ; load hex constant\nMOV BX, AX       ; copy AX -> BX\nMOV CL, 42       ; load decimal\nMOV DH, CL       ; copy 8-bit reg\nHLT\n; @expect AX = 0x1234\n; @expect BX = 0x1234\n; @expect CL = 42"
    },
    {
      "id": "explain",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "source": "## Key Points\n- `MOV dest, src` copies data (does not modify `src`)\n- Hex literals use `h` suffix (e.g. `1234h`)\n- Decimal literals have no suffix\n- You cannot MOV directly between two memory locations"
    },
    {
      "id": "code-2",
      "kind": "code",
      "source": ".CODE\nMOV AX, 0h       ; clear AX\nMOV AL, 0FFh     ; set low byte to 255\n; @expect AX = 0x00FF\n; @expect AL = 0xFF\nHLT"
    }
  ]
}
